ng that the  german government is not doing enough  to cut those emissions and she said that  for a wealthy and and big country like  germany this is not something that's  acceptable and she said that the people  are demanding change and that she hopes  that when there are enough people taking  to the streets that this change is  coming  anya tell us because as we've heard  there's more than 1500 demonstrations  happening all around the world today for  this cause and it's interesting to see  toonberg actually coming to germany  we know that the german federal election  is on sunday how much could this protest  impact the vote  well i talked to one of the organizers  of this demonstration of this  demonstration earlier and she said  that this does not have anything to do  with the german elections  she said that the date for this  demonstration and the strike worldwide  uh that it was set a long time ago and  that  you know it's a worldwide strike so it's  not it's not supposed to be a message to  german voters but of course it's hard to  believe that i mean this demonstration  has taken place right in front of the  german parliament and it's hard to  believe that this is not meant to be a  signal to those maybe still undecided  voters  that go to that have to cast their  ballot on sunday  and your german buddhist talk thank you  anya  cities like berlin and london are  regularly seeing tens of thousands of  people rally for action on climate  change  in poland the climate strike movement  struggled to get off the ground  one high school student took it upon  herself to change that  over the last two years she helped grow  the polish fridays for future into an  organization that has caught the  attention of people in power  they hope to bring some change to what  may be the biggest challenge in human  history  maya martina and emilia are climate  activists  this friday they are skipping school  again and taking to the streets to make  their voices heard  for maya th